        "problem": "Find the fourth-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{4}(x)$ of ${x}^{5/3}$ about $x=1.$\n\n${P}_{4}(x)=1\\,+\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$(x-1)\\,+\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n${(x-1)}^{2}\\,-\\,$\n{{field-3}}\n${(x-1)}^{3}\\,+\\,\\frac{5}{243}{(x-1)}^{4}$",
        "worked_solution": "The fourth-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{4}(x)= & \\,f(a)+{f}^{\u2032}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{\u2033}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{\u2034}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3} \\\\  & \\,+\\frac{{f}^{(4)}(a)}{4!}(x-a)^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $a=1,$ so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{4}(x)= & \\,f(1)+{f}^{\u2032}(1)(x-1)+\\frac{{f}^{\u2033}(1)}{2!}(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{\u2034}(1)}{3!}(x-1)^{3} \\\\  & \\,+\\frac{{f}^{(4)}(1)}{4!}(x-1)^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need to work out $f(1),$ ${f}^{\u2032}(1),$ ${f}^{\u2033}(1),$ ${f}^{\u2034}(1)$ and ${f}^{(4)}(1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{5/3} & \u27f9 &  & f(1) & ={1}^{5/3}=1 \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{5}{3}{x}^{2/3} & \u27f9 &  & {f}^{\u2032}(1) & =\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(x) & =\\frac{10}{9}{x}^{-1/3} & \u27f9 &  & {f}^{\u2033}(1) & =\\frac{10}{9} \\\\ {f}^{\u2034}(x) & =-\\frac{10}{27}{x}^{-4/3} & \u27f9 &  & {f}^{\u2034}(1) & =-\\frac{10}{27} \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(x) & =\\frac{40}{81}{x}^{-7/3} & \u27f9 &  & {f}^{(4)}(1) & =\\frac{40}{81}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${P}_{4}(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{4}(x) & =1+\\frac{5}{3}(x-1)+\\frac{10}{9\\cdot 2!}(x-1)^{2}-\\frac{10}{27\\cdot 3!}(x-1)^{3}+\\frac{40}{81\\cdot 4!}(x-1)^{4} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{5}{3}(x-1)+\\frac{5}{9}(x-1)^{2}-\\frac{5}{81}(x-1)^{3}+\\frac{5}{243}(x-1)^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
